      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded in 1848 by Oregonians mining at Big Bar, the town grew into one of California’s richest gold towns.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mokelumne_Hill%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from the Miwok “people of Mokel,” linked to the nearby Mokelumne River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mokelumne_Hill%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A wooden blacksmith shop now houses the town’s Library/Archives, preserving local history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.calaverashistory.org/mokelumne-hill",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The oldest Congregational Church in California, rebuilt after the 1854 fire, stands on French Hill.",
